Committed. 🐙

## What I did

Two mechanical repairs, both verified against the dispatcher before touching anything:

A — `/goal verify` (src/commands.rs): handle_goal routes verify, verify clear, verify <cmd>; /help goal documents all three; GOAL_SUBCOMMANDS didn't list it. Both discovery surfaces read that one const (hint_from! for the inline hint, filter_candidates for tab-completion), so a working documented feature was invisible to exactly the surfaces users find commands through. Added "verify" — nothing more, since #702 was the opposite failure (a hinted verb nothing implemented).

B — `/spawn manifest[s]` (src/help_data.rs): three usage lines matching what's actually routed (manifest/manifests → list, manifest <id> → show).

## The part worth reporting

Instance B already had a guard — `test_spawn_help_mentions_every_subcommand asserted help.contains(sub) for every entry, and it was **green**. It passed because "manifests" occurs in the entry's prose as a noun ("--parallel` run manifests"). The proof had settled at the layer cheapest to assert against: a container check standing in for the payload. I replaced it with one asserting the usage form /spawn <verb>, and confirmed it fails red against the pre-fix text.

## Verification

Both tests written first and observed failing for the right reasons before either fix (/goal verify … GOAL_SUBCOMMANDS omits it; /spawn help never writes /spawn manifest). After: 4737 passed, 1 ignored, 0 failed; cargo test --test module_size green; clippy --all-targets -D warnings clean; fmt applied.

src/commands_spawn.rs untouched — it sits at exactly its 3913-line recorded ceiling, and one added line would have reverted the whole task (that killed #719 and #721 today).

## What I did *not* do

The structural fix stays open, and I said so in the commit rather than letting the closure imply more than it earned: there are three hand-maintained copies per command and a drift test on exactly one of them (/git). Until the table-driven generalisation exists, the next drift gets found the way this one did — by accident. I skipped filing the follow-up issue (explicitly optional, and #722 remains open carrying the class).
